===Alternative forms===
* [[house fly]], [[house-fly]]

===Etymology===
From {{compound|house|fly|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|huisvlieg||housefly|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|husflue||housefly|lang=da}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|husfluga||housefly|lang=sv}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|houseflies}}

# Any [[fly]] regularly found in human dwellings.
## The [[common housefly]], ''[[Musca domestica]]'', that frequents most [[home]]s and spreads some [[disease]]s.
##* '''1990''', D. C. Kaslow, S. Welburn, ''16: Insect-transmitted pathogens in the insect midgut'', M. Lehane, P. Billingsley (editors), ''Biology of the Insect Midgut'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=AcFPDuUzE5kC&pg=PA454&dq=%22housefly%22%7C%22houseflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=fSZEUsKnMczxiAfgn4BQ&ved=0CK4BEOgBMBk#v=onepage&q=%22housefly%22%7C%22houseflies%22&f=false page 454],
##*: Of the three potential means (carriage on the body and legs, regurgitation and defecation) by which '''houseflies''' can transmit pathogens, one involves passage through the gut. During passage through the '''housefly''', pathogens may replicate within the gut.
##* '''2004''', R. Jurenka, ''Insect Pheromone Biosynthesis'', Stefan Schulz (editor), ''The Chemistry of Pheromones and Other Semiochemicals I'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=T1HrVzhjB8MC&pg=PA123&dq=%22housefly%22%7C%22houseflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=fSZEUsKnMczxiAfgn4BQ&ved=0CHQQ6AEwDg#v=onepage&q=%22housefly%22%7C%22houseflies%22&f=false page 123],
##*: In the '''housefly''', ''M. domestica'', sex pheromone production is correlated with egg development.
##* '''2011''', Ross Piper, ''Pests: A Guide to the World's Most Maligned, Yet Misunderstood Creatures'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=wRDIOhoQR68C&pg=PA102&dq=%22housefly%22%7C%22houseflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=fSZEUsKnMczxiAfgn4BQ&ved=0CJcBEOgBMBU#v=onepage&q=%22housefly%22%7C%22houseflies%22&f=false page 102],
##*: '''Houseflies''' are known to carry at least 100 different pathogens and they are vectors for at least 65 of these.

